+## 19. Syntax highlighting needs a theme of your own (2026-11-18)
+
+Local patch, not upstream. `tui-markdown`'s `highlight-code` feature is on (syntect, with
+a C oniguruma build), and `[ui] code_theme` names the `.tmTheme` it highlights with. It is
+loaded once, before the screen is drawn, into one set of render options that every block
+borrows, so no block clones a theme. A relative path is read from the config file's folder,
+`~/` from home. A file that does not load is reported by `plannotator-tui config` and leaves
+the review with plain code, as an unreadable config file always has.
+
+With the feature compiled in, `tui-markdown` highlights any recognised language and falls
+back to its own Base16 Ocean theme when given none; it has no switch to turn highlighting
+off. So without `code_theme`, every fence's info string is cut from the block's source
+before rendering (alignment still runs against the real source), no syntax is found, and
+code renders plain in `code-block`. The visible cost: the opening fence shows no language.
+Rendering code ourselves instead was rejected: we never interpret Markdown.
+
+`tui-markdown` silently omits a line it fails to highlight. A rendered code block has at
+least as many lines as its source, so a top-level code block that comes back shorter is
+rendered again without its language. No input was found that makes it drop a line
+(escape sequences, NUL and bare carriage returns all came back whole), so the guard has no
+test; it is two comparisons, kept because a lost line could not be seen or annotated. Highlighted lines carry only foreground colours on
+their spans and the source's own characters, so alignment maps them as before.
